[\n\\frac{3}{n+1} < \\sqrt{3} - 1 < \\frac{3}{n}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( \\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732 \\), we approximate \\( \\sqrt{3} - 1 \\approx 0.732 \\). This gives us the inequalities:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{3}{n+1} < 0.732 < \\frac{3}{n}\n\\]\n\nSolving the right inequality \\( 0.732 < \\frac{3}{n} \\):\n\n\\[\n0.732n < 3 \\implies n < \\frac{3}{0.732} \\approx 4.098 \\implies n \\leq 4\n\\]\n\nSolving the left inequality \\( \\frac{3}{n+1} < 0.732 \\):\n\n\\[\n3 < 0.732(n+1) \\implies \\frac{3}{0.732} < n + 1 \\implies 4.098 < n + 1 \\implies n > 3.098 \\implies n \\geq 4\n\\]\n\nCombining both results, \\( n \\) must be exactly 4. Verifying this, we compute:\n\n- For \\( n = 4 \\), \\( 1 + \\frac{3}{4} = 1.75 \\) and \\( 1 + \\frac{3}{5} = 1.6 \\)\n- Since \\( 1.6 < 1.732 < 1.75 \\), \\( \\sqrt{3} \\) is indeed between these values.\n\nThus, the value of \\( n \\) is \\(\\boxed{4}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Given the inequality \\( \\sqrt{3} \\) lies between \\( 1 + \\frac{3}{n} \\) and \\( 1 + \\frac{3}{n+1} \\), we can write:\n   \\[\n   1 + \\frac{3}{n+1} < \\sqrt{3} < 1 + \\frac{3}{n}\n   \\]\n\n2. Start by simplifying the right part of the inequality \\( \\sqrt{3} < 1 + \\frac{3}{n} \\):\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{3} - 1 < \\frac{3}{n}\n   \\]\n\n3. Solving for \\( n \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   n < \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}\n   \\]\n   Rationalize the denominator:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{(\\sqrt{3} - 1)(\\sqrt{3} + 1)} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{3 - 1} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   n < \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n4. Next, simplify the left part of the inequality \\( 1 + \\frac{3}{n+1} < \\sqrt{3} \\):\n   \\[\n   1 + \\frac{3}{n+1} < \\sqrt{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3}{n+1} < \\sqrt{3} - 1\n   \\]\n\n5. Solving for \\( n \\), we get:\n   \\[\n   n+1 > \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3} - 1}\n   \\]\n   Using the same rationalization as before:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3} - 1} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{3 - 1} = \\frac{3(\\sqrt{3} + 1)}{2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2}\n   \\]\n   Therefore:\n   \\[\n   n + 1 > \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   n > \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2} - 1 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 3 - 2}{2} = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 1}{2}\n   \\]\n\n6. Combining both conditions for \\( n \\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 1}{2} < n < \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2}\n   \\]\n\n7. Numerical approximation:\n   \\[\n   \\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732\n   \\]\n   First, calculate the upper bound:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 3}{2} \\approx \\frac{3(1.732) + 3}{2} = \\frac{5.196 + 3}{2} = \\frac{8.196}{2} \\approx 4.098\n   \\]\n   Then the lower bound:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{3\\sqrt{3} + 1}{2} \\approx \\frac{3(1.732) + 1}{2} = \\frac{5.196 + 1}{2} = \\frac{6.196}{2} \\approx 3.098\n   \\]\n\n8. Therefore, \\( n \\) is an integer within the interval:\n   \\[\n   3.098 < n < 4.098\n   \\]\n   Clearly, the only integer in this range is \\( n = 4 \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{4}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "46 countries sent teams to participate in the Asian Math Competition, which had a total of 4 problems. The results are as follows: 235 students got the first problem correct; 59 students got both the first and second problems correct; 29 students got both the first and third problems correct; 15 students got both the first and fourth problems correct; 3 students got all four problems correct. Additionally, it is known that some students got the first three problems correct but did not get the fourth problem correct.\n\nProve that there exists at least one country where at least 4 of its participants got only the first problem correct.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let's see. The problem is about proving that there exists at least one country where at least 4 of its participants got only the first problem correct. So, given that there are 46 countries, and 