udents who got problems 1,2,4 correct but not 3.\n\n- Let z be the number of students who got problems 1,3,4 correct but not 2.\n\n- Let w be the number of students who got problems 2,3,4 correct but not 1. But since we're only considering students who got problem 1 correct, maybe we don't need w.\n\nWait, actually, the students who got problem 1 correct can be partitioned into those who got only problem 1, those who got problem 1 and exactly one other, those who got problem 1 and exactly two others, and those who got problem 1 and three others (i.e., all four). So, maybe:\n\nTotal students with problem 1 correct = Only P1 + (P1&P2 only + P1&P3 only + P1&P4 only) + (P1&P2&P3 only + P1&P2&P4 only + P1&P3&P4 only) + All four.\n\nWe know the total is 235.\n\nWe know that the number of students who got all four correct is 3. So, that's the last term.\n\nAlso, the number of students who got P1&P2 correct is 59, but that includes those who might have also gotten P3 or P4. Similarly, P1&P3 is 29, and P1&P4 is 15. So, these numbers are for all students who got at least those two problems, possibly more.\n\nSo, for example, the 59 students who got P1&P2 correct include those who also got P3, P4, or both. Similarly for the others.\n\nTherefore, the number of students who got exactly P1&P2 correct would be 59 minus those who got P1&P2&P3, P1&P2&P4, and those who got all four.\n\nSimilarly, the number of students who got exactly P1&P3 correct is 29 minus those who got P1&P2&P3, P1&P3&P4, and all four.\n\nSimilarly, the number of students who got exactly P1&P4 correct is 15 minus those who got P1&P2&P4, P1&P3&P4, and all four.\n\nThen, the number of students who got exactly P1&P2&P3 correct would be x (given that some students got the first three correct but not the fourth). Similarly, the number of students who got exactly P1&P2&P4 correct would be something, but we don't have data on that. The problem only mentions that some students got the first three correct but not the fourth. It doesn't specify for other three-problem combinations.\n\nWait, this is getting complicated. Maybe we need to set up variables for the unknowns.\n\nLet me try to denote:\n\nLet a = number of students who got exactly P1.\n\nb = number who got exactly P1&P2.\n\nc = number who got exactly P1&P3.\n\nd = number who got exactly P1&P4.\n\ne = number who got exactly P1&P2&P3.\n\nf = number who got exactly P1&P2&P4.\n\ng = number who got exactly P1&P3&P4.\n\nh = number who got all four (h = 3).\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that e >= some value, but actually, the problem says \"some students got the first three problems correct but did not get the fourth problem correct,\" which means e >= 1.\n\nBut perhaps we don't need the exact value. Let's see.\n\nWe also know that:\n\nTotal students with P1 correct: a + b + c + d + e + f + g + h = 235.\n\nFrom the given data:\n\nNumber of students who got P1&P2 correct: b + e + f + h = 59.\n\nNumber of students who got P1&P3 correct: c + e + g + h = 29.\n\nNumber of students who got P1&P4 correct: d + f + g + h = 15.\n\nWe need to find a lower bound for a, the number of students who got only P1 correct.\n\nSo, let's solve these equations.\n\nFrom the first equation:\n\na = 235 - (b + c + d + e + f + g + h).\n\nBut we need to express b, c, d, e, f, g in terms of the given data.\n\nFrom the second equation: b + e + f + h = 59.\n\nSimilarly:\n\nc + e + g + h = 29.\n\nd + f + g + h = 15.\n\nWe have h = 3, so substitute h = 3 into the equations.\n\nSo:\n\nb + e + f + 3 = 59 => b + e + f = 56. (1)\n\nc + e + g + 3 = 29 => c + e + g = 26. (2)\n\nd + f + g + 3 = 15 => d + f + g = 12. (3)\n\nNow, let's try to express b, c, d in terms of e, f, g.\n\nFrom (1): b = 56 - e - f.\n\nFrom (2): c = 26 - e - g.\n\nFrom (3): d = 12 - f - g.\n\nNow, substitute these into the expression for a:\n\na = 235 - (b + c + d + e + f + g + h)\n\n= 235 - [(56 - e - f) + (26 - e - g) + (12 - f - g) + e + f + g + 3]\n\nLet me compute step by step:\n\nFi