ase the sum of squares. Therefore, the equally spaced symmetric configuration is likely optimal because it spreads the points as much as possible given the sum constraint.\n\nAlternatively, consider two points. For n=2, the maximum minimal distance m would be when the points are placed at sqrt(1/2) and -sqrt(1/2), giving m=2*sqrt(1/2)=sqrt(2). But the formula for n=2 would be sqrt(12/(2*(4 -1)))=sqrt(12/6)=sqrt(2). Correct. However, in our problem, n \u22653. So for n=2, which is not considered here, the formula still works.\n\nTherefore, the formula seems consistent. \n\nBut let's consider another case. For n=4, as above, the minimal distance m=1/\u221a5\u22480.447. If we tried to place the points differently, say, three points close together and one far apart, but keeping the minimal distance higher. For example, suppose three points at 0, m, 2m and one point at some position x. The minimal distance would be m, but to make sure that |x -2m| \u2265m and |x -0| \u2265m. So x needs to be \u2264 -m or \u22653m. If we place x at 3m, then the points are 0, m, 2m, 3m. Sum of squares is 0\u00b2 + m\u00b2 + (2m)\u00b2 + (3m)\u00b2=0 + m\u00b2 +4m\u00b2 +9m\u00b2=14m\u00b2. Set equal to 1: 14m\u00b2=1 => m=1/\u221a14\u22480.267. But the minimal distance here is m\u22480.267, which is less than the previous 0.447. Hence, this configuration is worse. Alternatively, place symmetrically: -3m, -m, m, 3m. Then sum of squares is 2*(9m\u00b2 +m\u00b2)=20m\u00b2. Setting equal to 1 gives m=1/sqrt(20)\u22480.223. The minimal distance is 2m\u22480.447. Wait, but here the minimal distance is 2m (distance between -3m and -m is 2m, etc.), but the minimal distance is 2m. Wait, so if we set the points as -3m, -m, m, 3m, the minimal distance is 2m. So sum of squares is 9m\u00b2 + m\u00b2 + m\u00b2 +9m\u00b2=20m\u00b2. Setting to 1: m=1/sqrt(20). Then minimal distance is 2m=2/sqrt(20)=sqrt(4/20)=sqrt(1/5)=1/\u221a5\u22480.447, which is same as the equally spaced case. But in this configurati