B) \\perp (ABCD)\\) and \\((CPD) \\perp (ABCD)\\), it implies that \\(PX \\perp (ABCD)\\). \n\n3. The angle between the planes \\(APB\\) and \\(CPD\\) is given by the dihedral angle between these planes, which is the same as the angle \\(BXC\\).\n\n4. From the problem statement, the sum of the angles \\(\\angle A\\) and \\(\\angle D\\) in the quadrilateral \\(ABCD\\) is given to be five times lesser than the sum of the angles \\(\\angle B\\) and \\(\\angle C\\). Therefore, \n   \\[\n   \\angle A + \\angle D = \\frac{1}{5} (\\angle B + \\angle C).\n   \\]\n\n5. In any quadrilateral, the sum of the interior angles is \\(360^\\circ\\). Hence, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\angle A + \\angle B + \\angle C + \\angle D = 360^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n6. Let \\(\\alpha = \\angle A + \\angle D\\) and \\(\\beta = \\angle B + \\angle C\\), then:\n   \\[\n   \\alpha + \\beta = 360^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   Given \\(\\alpha = \\frac{1}{5} \\beta\\), we substitute into the equation:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{5} \\beta + \\beta = 360^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\frac{6}{5} \\beta = 360^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\(\\beta\\), we get:\n   \\[\n   \\beta = 360^\\circ \\times \\frac{5}{6} = 300^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n7. Since \\(\\beta = \\angle B + \\angle C\\) and \\(\\alpha = \\frac{1}{5} \\beta\\):\n   \\[\n   \\alpha = \\frac{1}{5} \\times 300^\\circ = 60^\\circ.\n   \\]\n   Thus \\(\\angle A + \\angle D = 60^\\circ\\).\n\n8. The linear angle \\(BXC\\) being formed is given by the dihedral angle across \\(PX\\):\n\n   Since \\(PX \\perp (ABCD)\\), the dihedral angle at edge \\(PX\\) between two intersecting planes is supplementary to \\(\\angle A + \\angle D\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\angle BXC = 180^\\circ - (\\angle A + \\angle D) = 180^\\circ - 60^\\circ = 120^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\n9. The required angle between the planes is \\(\\angle BXC = 120^\\circ\\). However, since this angle is obtuse, the acute angle between the planes is given by:\n   \\[\n   180^\\circ - 120^\\circ = 60^\\circ.\n   \\]\n\nConclusi