erlap is D0=73.5. But D0=73.5 is within the first interval [72.5,73.5] and third interval [72.5,73.5], and also in the second interval [73.5,74.5]. Since all intervals are closed, D0=73.5 is included in all three.\n\nTherefore, D0=73.5 is possible. Then, check the distances:\n\nAt 12:00: D0=73.5 km, which Masha rounded to 73 (she could have chosen 74, but she chose 73).\n\nAt 12:15: D(0.25)=73.5 -0.25*48=73.5 -12=61.5 km, which is exactly 61.5. Masha rounds to 62? Wait, but 61.5 can be rounded to 61 or 62. But Masha reported 62, so she chose to round up.\n\nAt 12:45: D(0.75)=73.5 -0.75*48=73.5 -36=37.5 km. Similarly, 37.5 can be rounded to 37 or 38. Masha reported 37, so she chose to round down.\n\nBut the problem doesn't state that she rounds in a consistent way; she can choose either when it's a half-integer. Therefore, this scenario is possible. Therefore, v=48 is a valid solution.\n\nIs there another possible speed?\n\nLet\u2019s check if there are other possible speeds. Suppose v is slightly less than 48, say v=47. Then:\n\nFrom the first inequality: D0 \u2208 [72.5,73.5]\n\nFrom the second inequality: D0 \u2208 [61.5 +0.25*47=61.5+11.75=73.25,62.5+11.75=74.25]\n\nFrom the third inequality: D0 \u2208 [36.5 +0.75*47=36.5+35.25=71.75,37.5+35.25=72.75]\n\nSo overlapping intervals:\n\nFirst: [72.5,73.5]\n\nSecond: [73.25,74.25]\n\nThird: [71.75,72.75]\n\nThe intersection of these would be [73.25,72.75], which is empty. Therefore, no solution.\n\nIf we try v=48. Let\u2019s also check v=49.333... (which was 37.5 +0.75v=73.5 \u2192v=(73.5 -36.5)/0.75=37/0.75\u224849.333). But v=49.333 would be:\n\nSecond inequality: D0 \u2208 [61.5 +0.25*49.333\u224861.5 +12.333\u224873.833,62.5 +12.333\u224874.833]\n\nThird inequality: D0 \u2208 [36.5 +0.75*49.333\u224836.5 +37\u224873.5,37.5 +37\u224874.5]\n\nFirst inequality: [72.5,73.5]\n\nThe intersection would be [73.5,73.5] again, but since v=49.333 exceeds the previous constraints from the first two inequalities (which required v \u226448), this is invalid.\n\nWait, but let's check the first two inequalities for v=49.333:\n\nFrom first two inequalities:\n\n72.5 \u2264 D0 \u226473.5\n\n61.5 +0.25v \u2264 D0 \u226462.5 +0.25v\n\nAt v=49.333, 61.5 +0.25*49.333\u224861.5 +12.333\u224873.833, which is higher than the first interval's upper bound of73.5. Therefore, no overlap. Hence, no solution.\n\nTherefore, the only possible speed is v=48 km/h.\n\nBut let's confirm with D0=73.5:\n\nAt 12:00: 73.5 km, rounds to 73.\n\nAt 12:15: 73.5 -0.25*48=73.5 -12=61.5 km, rounds to 62.\n\nAt 12:45:73.5 -0.75*48=73.5 -36=37.5 km, rounds to 37.\n\nAll match the reported distances. Therefore, v=48 km/h is a solution.\n\nIs there any other speed?\n\nSuppose there is a speed where all three distances are not half-integers, so that Masha's rounding is uniquely determined. For example, suppose D0=73.0, v=48.\n\nThen:\n\nAt 12:00:73.0 \u219273\n\nAt 12:15:73 -12=61 \u219261, but Masha reported 62. So that's not possible. So this case is invalid.\n\nAlternatively, if D0=73.5, v=48, as above.\n\nAnother case: Suppose D0=72.5, v=48.\n\nAt 12:00:72.5, rounds to 73 (correct).\n\nAt 12:15:72.5 -12=60.5, rounds to 60 or 61. But Masha reported 62. So no, this doesn't work.\n\nSimilarly, D0=73.5 is the only value that works. Therefore, v=48 km/h.\n\nWait, but let's check another possibility. Suppose the speed is slightly different, say v=47.5.\n\nCompute D0 ranges:\n\nFirst interval:72.5 \u2264D0 \u226473.5\n\nSecond interval:61.5 +0.25*47.5=61.5 +11.875=73.375 \u2264D0 \u226462.5 +11.875=74.375\n\nThird interval:36.5 +0.75*47.5=36.5 +35.625=72.125 \u2264D0 \u226437.5 +35.625=73.125\n\nSo intersection would be max(72.5,73.375,72.125)=73.375 \u2264D0 \u2264min(73.5,74.375,73.125)=73.125\n\nBut 73.375 >73.125, so no solution.\n\nTherefore, no overlap. Hence, v=48 is the only possible speed.\n\nAnother check: Let\u2019s see if there's a speed where D0 -0.25v and D0 -0.75v are both integers, so rounding is not needed.\n\nSuppose D0=73, v=48.\n\nThen:\n\nAt 12:00:73 km (exact)\n\nAt 12:15:73 -12=61 km (exact), but Masha reported 62. So this doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, D0=74, v=48.\n\nBut D0=74 is outside the first interval [72.5,73.5].\n\nAlternatively, D0=73.5, v=48:\n\nAt 12:00:73.5 \u2192rounded to73\n\nAt 12:15:61.5 \u2192rounded to62\n\nAt 12:45:37.5 \u2192rounded to37\n\nThis works, and the speed is 48 km/h.\n\nIs there any other speed? Suppose we take a different D0. For example, suppose D0=73.0, and the speed is such that the distances at 12:15 and 12:45 are also within the required rounded intervals.\n\nSo D0=73.0.\n\nThen at 12:15: D=73 -0.25v must be in [61.5,62.5]\n\nSo 73 -0.25v \u2208 [61.5,62.5]\n\nSo 61.5 \u226473 -0.25v \u226462.5\n\n=> 73 -62.5 \u22640.25v \u226473 -61.5\n\n=> 10.5 \u22640.25v \u226411.5\n\n=> 42 \u2264v \u226446\n\nSimilarly, at 12:45: D=73 -0.75v must be in [36.5,37.5]\n\nSo 36.5 \u226473 -0.75v \u226437.5\n\n=>73 -37.5 \u22640.75v \u226473 -36.5\n\n=>35.5 \u22640.75v \u226436.5\n\n=>35.5/0.75 \u224847.333 \u2264v \u226436.5/0.75 \u224848.666...\n\nSo from the first condition (42 \u2264v \u226446) and the second condition (\u224847.333 \u2264v \u2264\u224848.666), there is no overlap. Hence, no solution with D0=73.0.\n\nSimilarly, if we take D0=72.5:\n\nAt 12:15:72.5 -0.25v \u2208[61.5,62.5]\n\n=>72.5 -62.5 \u22640.25v \u226472.5 -61.5\n\n=>10 \u22640.25v \u226411\n\n=>40 \u2264v \u226444\n\nAt 12:45:72.5 -0.75v \u2208[36.5,37.5]\n\n=>72.5 -37.5 \u22640.75v \u226472.5 -36.5\n\n=>35 \u22640.75v \u226436\n\n=>46.666... \u2264v \u226448\n\nAgain, no overlap between 40-44 and46.666-48. Hence, no solution.\n\nTherefore, the only possible speed is 48 km/h, which corresponds to D0=73.5 km. This satisfies all the rounding conditions because Masha can choose to round 73.5 to73, 61.5 to62, and37.5 to37. \n\nThus, the speed of the train is 48 km/h.", "deepseek_solution": "The speed of the train is **48 km/h**. Here's the detailed reasoning:\n\n1. **Define V