blem into counting the number of ways to distribute indistinguishable objects (stars) into distinguishable bins (bars).\n\n    Define new variables \\(x_0, x_1, \\ldots, x_9\\) such that:\n    \\[\n    a = 0 \\cdot x_0 + 1 \\cdot x_1 + \\cdots + 9 \\cdot x_9\n    \\]\n    This can be visualized as partitioning three units (representing \\(a, b, c\\)) into 10 bins (representing each digit from 0 to 9).\n\n3. **Using the stars and bars theorem:**\n\n    The stars and bars theorem states that the number of ways to distribute \\(n\\) indistinguishable stars into \\(k\\) distinguishable bins is given by:\n    \\[\n    \\binom{n + k - 1}{k - 1}\n    \\]\n    Here, \\(n\\) is the total number of stars (3 digits), and \\(k\\) is the number of bins (10 possible digits).\n\n    So, the number of different codes is:\n    \\[\n    \\binom{3 + 10 - 1}{10 - 1} = \\binom{12}{9}\n    \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the value of \\(\\binom{12}{9}\\):**\n\n    Using the symmetric property of binomial coefficients \\(\\binom{n}{k} = \\binom{n}{n-k}\\), we get:\n    \\[\n    \\binom{12}{9} = \\binom{12}{3}\n    \\]\n    Now we calculate:\n    \\[\n    \\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12!}{3!(12-3)!} = \\frac{12!}{3!9!}\n    \\]\n\n5. **Simplify the factorial expression:**\n\n    \\[\n    \\binom{12}{3} = \\frac{12 \\times 11 \\times 10}{3 \\times 2 \\times 1} = 220\n    \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe number of different three-digit codes that Angelika can choose, where each digit is in non-decreasing order, is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{220}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Let $A_{1} A_{2} \\ldots A_{9}=K_{9}$ be a regular nonagon with center $O$. The midpoints of the sides $A_{1} A_{2}$ and $A_{4} A_{5}$ are $B_{1}$ and $B_{4}$, respectively. Show that the lines $A_{1} A_{4}$, $B_{1} B_{4}$, and $AO_{7}$ intersect at a single point.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I have this geometry problem here about a regular nonagon. Let me try to vi