u00b2 = n\u2074 + 6n\u00b3 + 11n\u00b2 + 6n + 1. The given polynomial is n\u2074 + 6n\u00b3 + 11n\u00b2 + 3n + 31. Subtract these: (given) - (square) = (3n + 31) - (6n + 1) = -3n + 30. So, the given polynomial is (n\u00b2 + 3n + 1)\u00b2 - 3n + 30. Hmm, interesting. So we can write:\n\nn\u2074 + 6n\u00b3 + 11n\u00b2 + 3n + 31 = (n\u00b2 + 3n + 1)\u00b2 - 3n + 30.\n\nWe want this to be a perfect square. Let's denote S = n\u00b2 + 3n + 1, so the expression becomes S\u00b2 - 3n + 30. We need S\u00b2 - 3n + 30 = k\u00b2 for some integer k.\n\nMaybe we can bound this between two consecutive squares. Let's see. Suppose that k is close to S. Let's write k = S - t for some small integer t. Then:\n\nk\u00b2 = (S - t)\u00b2 = S\u00b2 - 2tS + t\u00b2.\n\nSet this equal to S\u00b2 - 3n + 30:\n\nS\u00b2 - 2tS + t\u00b2 = S\u00b2 - 3n + 30 \u21d2 -2tS + t\u00b2 = -3n + 30.\n\nRearranged: 2tS - t\u00b2 = 3n - 30.\n\nBut S = n\u00b2 + 3n + 1, so substitute:\n\n2t(n\u00b2 + 3n + 1) - t\u00b2 = 3n - 30.\n\nThis seems a bit complicated, but maybe for small t, we can solve for n. Let's try t = 1:\n\n2*1*(n\u00b2 + 3n + 1) - 1 = 3n - 30 \u21d2 2n\u00b2 + 6n + 2 - 1 = 3n - 30 \u21d2 2n\u00b2 + 6n + 1 = 3n - 30 \u21d2 2n\u00b2 + 3n + 31 = 0. The discriminant is 9 - 248 = -239 < 0, so no real solutions.\n\nt = 2:\n\n2*2*(n\u00b2 + 3n + 1) - 4 = 3n - 30 \u21d2 4n\u00b2 + 12n + 4 - 4 = 3n - 30 \u21d2 4n\u00b2 + 12n = 3n - 30 \u21d2 4n\u00b2 + 9n + 30 = 0. Discriminant: 81 - 480 = -399 < 0. No solution.\n\nt = 3:\n\n2*3*(n\u00b2 + 3n + 1) - 9 = 3n - 30 \u21d2 6n\u00b2 + 18n + 6 -9 = 3n -30 \u21d2 6n\u00b2 + 18n -3 = 3n -30 \u21d2 6n\u00b2 +15n +27=0 \u21d2 Divide by 3: 2n\u00b2 +5n +9=0. Discriminant:25 -72= -47 <0. No solution.\n\nt = 0:\n\n2*0*S -0 = 3n -30 \u21d2 0 = 3n -30 \u21d2 3n =30 \u21d2n=10. Wait, so t=0 would imply k=S. So k\u00b2 = S\u00b2, so S\u00b2 -3n +30 = S\u00b2 \u21d2 -3n +30=0 \u21d2n=10. So n=10 is a solution. Let's check: n=10. Compute the polynomial: 10\u2074 +6*10\u0