t d1 and d2 are two integers such that d1*d2 =3(n -10). Then:\n\nx = (d1 + d2)/2,\n\nbut x =n\u00b2 +3n +1. So:\n\nn\u00b2 +3n +1 = (d1 + d2)/2.\n\nAlso, from d1*d2 =3(n -10), we can express n = (d1*d2)/3 +10. Substitute into the equation for x:\n\nn\u00b2 +3n +1 = (d1 + d2)/2.\n\nBut n is expressed in terms of d1 and d2. Let's substitute n:\n\n[(d1*d2)/3 +10]^2 +3[(d1*d2)/3 +10] +1 = (d1 + d2)/2.\n\nThis seems complicated, but maybe manageable. Let\u2019s denote k = d1*d2, so n = k/3 +10.\n\nThen the left-hand side becomes:\n\n(k/3 +10)^2 +3(k/3 +10) +1 = (k\u00b2/9 + 20k/3 +100) + (k +30) +1 = k\u00b2/9 +20k/3 +100 +k +30 +1 = k\u00b2/9 +23k/3 +131.\n\nSet this equal to (d1 + d2)/2. But d1 + d2 = ?\n\nSince d1*d2 =k, and d1 +d2 = ?\n\nBut this seems like a dead end. Maybe instead consider that d1 and d2 are divisors of 3(n -10). Let\u2019s think in terms of possible small divisors. Let\u2019s note that since d1 and d2 multiply to 3(n -10), and their sum is 2x = 2(n\u00b2 +3n +1). Let\u2019s see:\n\nLet me write:\n\nd1 + d2 = 2x = 2(n\u00b2 +3n +1),\n\nd1*d2 =3(n -10).\n\nSo, we have a system:\n\nd1 + d2 = 2n\u00b2 +6n +2,\n\nd1*d2 =3n -30.\n\nLet me denote s = d1 + d2 =2n\u00b2 +6n +2,\n\np =d1*d2=3n -30.\n\nSo, we have two equations:\n\ns =2n\u00b2 +6n +2,\n\np=3n -30.\n\nBut also, for integers d1 and d2, the quadratic equation t\u00b2 -st +p=0 must have integer roots. The discriminant must be a perfect square:\n\ns\u00b2 -4p = (2n\u00b2 +6n +2)\u00b2 -4*(3n -30).\n\nCompute this discriminant:\n\n(2n\u00b2 +6n +2)\u00b2 -12n +120.\n\nThis must be a perfect square. Let's expand (2n\u00b2 +6n +2)\u00b2:\n\n=4n\u2074 +24n\u00b3 + (36n\u00b2 +8n\u00b2) +24n +4. Wait, wait, let me do it step by step:\n\n(2n\u00b2 +6n +2)^2 = (2n\u00b2)^2 +2*(2n\u00b2)*(6n) +2*(2n\u00b2)*2 + (6n)^2 +2*(6n)*2 +2\u00b2\n\nWait, no, better to use (a + b + c)^2 = a\u00b2 + b\u00b2 + c\u00b2 +2ab +2ac +2bc. Here a=2n\u00b2, b=6n, c=2.\n\nSo,\n\n(2n\u00b2)^2 + (6n)^2 + (2)^2 +2*(2n\u00b2)*(6