tersect each of these positive edges at a point different from O. Therefore, points A, B, C must lie on the positive axes (i.e., a, b, c > 0). Therefore, in that case, a, b, c are positive real numbers.\n\nWait, this is a crucial point. The edges of the trihedral angle are the positive x, y, z axes (assuming the trihedral angle is in the first octant). Therefore, the sphere must intersect each of these positive edges at a point other than O. Therefore, points A, B, C are on the positive axes, so a, b, c are positive. Therefore, in equation (1), a, b, c are positive real numbers satisfying a n_x + b n_y + c n_z = ||N||\u00b2.\n\nTherefore, the centroids (\u03b1, \u03b2, \u03b3) = (a/3, b/3, c/3) would have positive coordinates, lying on the plane \u03b1 n_x + \u03b2 n_y + \u03b3 n_z = ||N||\u00b2 / 3, with \u03b1, \u03b2, \u03b3 > 0. Therefore, the locus is the portion of the plane in the first octant.\n\nBut wait, this depends on the position of point N. If N is in a different octant, the trihedral angle edges might be in different directions. Wait, the problem statement just mentions a trihedral angle with vertex O. It doesn't specify the orientation. So, perhaps the trihedral angle could be in any orientation, not necessarily aligned with the coordinate axes. However, to solve the problem, we can choose a coordinate system where the trihedral angle is along the positive axes, which is a common approach to simplify problems.\n\nBut let's confirm whether the trihedral angle's orientation affects the result. Suppose the trihedral angle is formed by three arbitrary edges emanating from O, not necessarily orthogonal. Then, the answer might be more complex. However, in the problem statement, it's just a trihedral angle, so three edges meeting at O. Without loss of generality, we can assign a coordinate system where the edges are the positive x, y, z axes. Because any trihedral angle can be transformed to this coordinate system via linear transformation, which preserves 