 to } ON \\text{ at one-third the distance from } O \\text{ to } N}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "1. Denote the unit vectors directed along the edges of the trihedral angle as $\\boldsymbol{a}, \\boldsymbol{b},$ and $\\boldsymbol{c}$. Assume $\\overrightarrow{ON} = \\boldsymbol{e}$ represents the vector from point $O$ to point $N$, and let $P$ be the center of the sphere. Thus, $\\overrightarrow{OP} = \\boldsymbol{u}$.\n2. Express vectors $\\overrightarrow{OA}, \\overrightarrow{OB}$, and $\\overrightarrow{OC}$ as:\n    \\[\n    \\overrightarrow{OA} = x\\boldsymbol{a}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{OB} = y\\boldsymbol{b}, \\quad \\overrightarrow{OC} = z\\boldsymbol{c}\n    \\]\n3. The points $O, N, A, B$, and $C$ reside on the same sphere with center $P$. This gives us the equations:\n    \\[\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        (\\boldsymbol{u} - \\boldsymbol{e})^2 &= \\boldsymbol{u}^2, \\\\\n        (x\\boldsymbol{a} - \\boldsymbol{u})^2 &= \\boldsymbol{u}^2, \\\\\n        (y\\boldsymbol{b} - \\boldsymbol{u})^2 &= \\boldsymbol{u}^2, \\\\\n        (z\\boldsymbol{c} - \\boldsymbol{u})^2 &= \\boldsymbol{u}^2 \n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\]\n4. Expanding these equations results in:\n    \\[\n    \\begin{aligned}\n        \\boldsymbol{e}^2 - 2\\boldsymbol{e} \\cdot \\boldsymbol{u} &= 0, \\\\\n        x^2\\boldsymbol{a}^2 - 2x\\boldsymbol{a} \\cdot \\boldsymbol{u} &= 0, \\\\\n        y^2\\boldsymbol{b}^2 - 2y\\boldsymbol{b} \\cdot \\boldsymbol{u} &= 0, \\\\\n        z^2\\boldsymbol{c}^2 - 2z\\boldsymbol{c} \\cdot \\boldsymbol{u} &= 0\n    \\end{aligned}\n    \\]\n5. Assuming the unit vectors $\\boldsymbol{a}, \\boldsymbol{b}$, and $\\boldsymbol{c}$ have unity magnitude, we define $\\boldsymbol{e}$ as a linear combination of these unit vectors:\n    \\[\n    \\boldsymbol{e} = \\alpha \\boldsymbol{a} + \\beta \\boldsymbol{b} + \\gamma \\boldsymbol{c}\n    \\]\n6. Substituting into the system of equations and multiplying each respective equation by $\\alpha, \\beta,$ and $\\gamma$, and