ts, 65 \\). Solving \\( 3a \\leq 65 \\) gives \\( a \\leq 21.666 \\), so the largest integer \\( a \\) is 21. Checking multiples, \\( 21 \\times 2 = 42 \\) and \\( 21 \\times 3 = 63 \\), both within the range.\n\n5. **Verification**: Including 21 in any subset forces the exclusion of 42 and 63, reducing the antichain size. Constructing a 33-element antichain with 21 is impossible, confirming that every 33-element subset containing 21 must be a good set.\n\nThus, the largest \\( a \\) such that every 33-element subset containing \\( a \\) is a good set is \\(\\boxed{21}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given $M = \\{1, 2, \\ldots, 65\\}$ and need to find the largest $a \\in M$ such that any subset $A \\subseteq M$ with $|A| = 33$ and containing $a$ forms a \"good\" set. A set $A$ is defined as good if for any $x, y \\in A$, $x < y$, and $x \\mid y$ is satisfied.\n\nTo solve this, follow the steps:\n\n1. **Define set \\( P \\):**\n   \\[\n   P = \\{21 + i \\mid i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 44\\} \\setminus \\{2(21 + i) \\mid i = 1, 2, \\ldots, 11\\}\n   \\]\n   Here, the set $P$ ensures $|P| = 33$.\n\n2. **Prove \\( P \\) is not a good set:**\n   We must show that within \\( P \\), there is no $i, j$ such that $1 \\leq i < j \\leq 44$ and $21 + j = n(21 + i)$ for some integer $n \\geq 3$. This non-existence means \\( P \\) cannot have a divisor relationship for forming a good set.\n\n3. **Consider the maximum value of \\( a \\):**\n   From the constructed set \\( P \\), it is clear that:\n   \\[\n   a \\leq 21\n   \\]\n   since for larger values contained in any 33-element subset, it will not ensure forming a good set.\n   \n4. **Verification of 21:**\n   We need to show that any subset of \\( M \\) containing 21 and having 33 elements forms a good set:\n   - Define \\( A = \\{a_1, a_2, \\ldots, a_{32}, 21\\} \\).\n\n5. **Element constraints:**\n   Determine the elements $1, 3, 7, 42, 63$:\n   - If any of them is in \\( A \\), then \\( A \\) must be good since including 21 with these values does not violate the \"good set\" property by direct inspection.\n\n6. **Collection of sets:**\n   Group elements to avoid multiplication relationships:\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   A_1 & = \\{2, 4, 8, 16, 32, 64\\} \\\\\n   A_2 & = \\{5, 10, 20, 40\\} \\\\\n   A_3 & = \\{6, 12, 24, 48\\} \\\\\n   A_4 & = \\{9, 18, 36\\} \\\\\n   A_5 & = \\{11, 22, 44\\} \\\\\n   A_6 & = \\{13, 26, 52\\} \\\\\n   A_7 & = \\{14, 28, 56\\} \\\\\n   A_8 & = \\{15, 30, 60\\} \\\\\n   A_9 & = \\{17, 34\\} \\\\\n   A_{10} & = \\{19, 38\\} \\\\\n   A_{11} & = \\{23, 46\\} \\\\\n   A_{12} & = \\{25, 50\\} \\\\\n   A_{13} & = \\{27, 54\\} \\\\\n   A_{14} & = \\{29, 58\\} \\\\\n   A_{15} & = \\{31, 62\\} \\\\\n   A' & = \\{33, 35, 37, \\ldots, 61, 65\\}\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n7. **Application of the Pigeonhole Principle:**\n   With the above sets, if 16 elements are chosen from \\( A' \\) where no multiplicative relationship exists, at least one subset from $A_1, A_2, \\ldots, A_{15}$ has to borrow elements, guaranteeing two elements with a multiplicative relationship will be included in \\( A \\). \n\n8. **Conclusion:**\n   Thus, any subset $A$ containing 21 will form a good set, making:\n   \\[\n   a_{\\text{max}} = 21\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{21}\n   \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find the value of the constant \\( c \\) so that the function \\( f(x) = \\arctan \\frac{2-2x}{1+4x} + c \\) is an odd function on the interval \\(\\left(-\\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the constant \\( c \\) such that the function \\( f(x) = \\arctan \\frac{2 - 2x}{1 + 4x} + c \\) is an odd function on the interval \\( \\left(-\\frac{1}{4}, \\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\). Hmm, let me recall what an odd function is. An odd function satisfies the property \\( f(-x) = -f(x) \\) for all \\( x \\) in its domain. So, essentially, if I plug in \\( -x \\) into 