 n) = n/p\n\nTherefore,\n\np = n(b - n)/(a - m)\n\nSimilarly, from the second condition angle MND + angle MBC = 180\u00b0, we can derive another equation.\n\nAngle MND is at N(p,n) between M(m,n), N(p,n), D(0,b). This is the angle between vectors NM and ND.\n\nVector NM = (m - p, 0), vector ND = (-p, b - n).\n\nThe angle between these vectors:\n\ncos(angle MND) = (NM \u22c5 ND) / (|NM| |ND|)\n\nNM \u22c5 ND = (m - p)(-p) + 0*(b - n) = -p(m - p)\n\n|NM| = |m - p|\n\n|ND| = sqrt(p^2 + (b - n)^2)\n\nThus,\n\ncos(angle MND) = -p(m - p)/( |m - p| sqrt(p^2 + (b - n)^2) )\n\nSince m > p, m - p > 0, so:\n\ncos(angle MND) = -p / sqrt(p^2 + (b - n)^2)\n\nTherefore, angle MND = arccos(-p / sqrt(p^2 + (b - n)^2)) = 180\u00b0 - arctan((b - n)/p)\n\nSimilarly, angle MBC is at B(a,0) between M(m,n), B(a,0), C(a,b). This is the angle between vectors BM and BC.\n\nVector BM = (m - a, n - 0) = (m - a, n)\n\nVector BC = (0, b - 0) = (0, b)\n\nAngle at B:\n\ncos(angle MBC) = (BM \u22c5 BC) / (|BM| |BC|)\n\nBM \u22c5 BC = (m - a)*0 + n*b = nb\n\n|BM| = sqrt((m - a)^2 + n^2)\n\n|BC| = b\n\nThus,\n\ncos(angle MBC) = nb / (b sqrt((m - a)^2 + n^2)) ) = n / sqrt((m - a)^2 + n^2)\n\nTherefore, angle MBC = arccos(n / sqrt((m - a)^2 + n^2)).\n\nSimilarly, this angle can be expressed as arctan((a - m)/n), since:\n\ncos(angle) = n / sqrt((m - a)^2 + n^2) \u21d2 adjacent = n, hypotenuse = sqrt((a - m)^2 + n^2) \u21d2 opposite = a - m \u21d2 tan(angle) = (a - m)/n \u21d2 angle = arctan((a - m)/n).\n\nGiven the second condition angle MND + angle MBC = 180\u00b0:\n\n180\u00b0 - arctan((b - n)/p) + arctan((a - m)/n) = 180\u00b0\n\nSimplifying:\n\narctan((a - m)/n) = arctan((b - n)/p)\n\nTaking tangent of both sides:\n\n(a - m)/n = (b - n)/p\n\nTherefore,\n\np = n(b - n)/(a - m)\n\nWhich is the same equation obtained from the first condition.\n\nTherefore, both angle conditions reduce to the same equation:\n\np = n(b - n)/(a - m)\n\nThis means that given M(m,n), the coordinates of N(p,n) must satisfy p