f we have points with coordinates (x_i, y_i), then for any two points, (x_i - x_j)^2 + (y_i - y_j)^2 = d_{ij}^2, where d_{ij} is integer. So, an infinite system of such equations. Maybe this can only be satisfied if all y_i = 0, i.e., points on the x-axis.\n\nBut how to show that?\n\nSuppose that there exists a point with y \u2260 0. Let's say we have a point P(x, y) with y \u2260 0. Then, the distances from P to all other points must be integers. So, for any other point Q(a, 0) on the line (assuming some points are on the line), the distance PQ is sqrt((x - a)^2 + y^2) must be integer. So, for each a, (x - a)^2 + y^2 must be a perfect square.\n\nBut if there are infinitely many points on the line, then we have infinitely many a's such that (x - a)^2 + y^2 is a perfect square. But this is equivalent to saying that there are infinitely many integers a such that (a - x)^2 + y^2 is a perfect square.\n\nBut this is like finding infinitely many integers a where (a - x)^2 + y^2 is a square. Let me set z = a - x, then we have z\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = k\u00b2, where z is an integer (if x is rational, maybe). Wait, but if x is not rational, then z = a - x might not be integer even if a is integer.\n\nWait, but if we assume that all points are on the x-axis except P(x,y), then the other points are at (a, 0) with a integer (if we set the line as the x-axis). Then x can be any real number, but for (x - a)^2 + y^2 to be a perfect square for infinitely many integers a.\n\nBut how often can this happen? For a fixed y, we can think of the equation z\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = k\u00b2, which is a Pythagorean triple. There are only finitely many z for a fixed y that satisfy this. Wait, no. For example, if y is a multiple of some number, you can have infinitely many Pythagorean triples. Wait, but Pythagorean triples are generated by parameters, but for a fixed y, there are only finitely many solutions z, k.\n\nWait, actually, if y is fixed, then z\u00b2 + y\u00b2 = k\u00b2 implies that k\u00b2 - z\u00b2 = y\u00b2, so (k - z)(k + z) = y\u00b2. Since k and z are integers, the product of two integers (k - z) and (k + z) is y\u00b2. The number of factorizations of y\u00b2 into two integers of the same parity (since k - z and k + z must both be even or both be odd, but since their product is y\u00b2, which is a square, they have to both be even if y is even or both be odd if y is odd). So, the number of solutions would correspond to the number of ways to factor y\u00b2 into two such integers, which is finite. Therefore, for fixed y, there are only finitely many z such that z\u00b2 + y\u00b2 is a perfect square. Therefore, there can only be finitely many a's such that (a - x)^2 + y\u00b2 is a perfect square. Therefore, if we have infinitely many points on the line, there can be at most finitely many points off the line.\n\nTherefore, if the entire set is infinite, it cannot have infinitely many points off the line, because for each off-line point, there are only finitely many points on the line that can be at an integer distance to it. Hence, if the set is infinite, all but finitely many must lie on the line. But the problem states that the entire set is infinite, so if infinitely many are on the line, can we have any points off the line?\n\nSuppose there is at least one point off the line. Then, as per above, there can only be finitely many points on the line that are at integer distances to it. But the set is infinite, so there must be infinitely many points on the line. However, those infinitely many points on the line would have to be at integer distances to the off-line point, which we just saw is impossible because there can only be finitely many. Therefore, this leads to a contradiction. Hence, there can be no points off the line, so all points must lie on a single line.\n\nWait, that seems like a valid argument. Let me recap:\n\n1. Assume there is an infinite set of points with all pairwise distances integers.\n2. Suppose not all points are colinear. Then, there exists at least three non-colinear points: A, B on a line, and C off the line.\n3. Any other point D must be at integer distances from A, B, and C.\n4. For point C off the line AB, consider the distances from C to all points on AB. If there are infinitely many points on AB, then for each such point, the distance to C must be an integer.\n5. However, as shown earlier, for a fixed point C off the line AB, there can only be finitely many points on AB that are at integer distances from C. This is because the equation (a - x)^2 + y^2 = k\u00b2 (where (x,y) are coordinates of C and a is the coordinate on AB) has only finitely many integer solutions a for fixed x and y.\n6. Therefore, if there are infinitely many points on AB, there must be infinitely many a's such that (a - x)^2 + y^2 is a perfect square, which is impossible. Hence, a contradiction.\n7. Therefore, our assumption that there exists a point C off the line AB must be false. Hence, all points lie on a single line.\n\nThat seems to make sense. So the crux is that a point off the line can only have finitely many points on the line at integer distance from it, hence if there are infinitely many points on the line, there can't be any points off the line. Therefore, all points must be colinear.\n\nLet me check if there's any loophole in this argument. Suppose point C is at (x, y), and we have infinitely many points on the line AB (say the x-axis) at positions (a_i, 0) where a_i are integers. Then the distance from each (a_i, 0) to (x, y) is sqrt((a_i - x)^2 + y^2). For this to be an integer for all i, we need infinitely many integers a_i such that (a_i - x)^2 + y^2 is a perfect square. But as per number theory, for fixed x and y, this equation can have only finitely many solutions a_i. Hence, such a set cannot be infinite. Therefore, if the entire set is infinite, there cannot be any such point C off the line, so all points must be on AB.\n\nTherefore, the conclusion is that all points must lie on a single line.\n\nI think this is a solid argument. Let me see if I can find any references or if this is a known result. I recall that this is indeed a known result, often proved using the rational distance argument as above. So, putting it all together, the proof hinges on showing that a non-colinear point would restrict the number of points on the line to be finite, contradicting the infiniteness of the set. Hence, all points must be colinear.", "deepseek_solution": "To prove that an infinite set of points with all pairwise distances being integers must lie on a single line, we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1: Assume the contrary.**  \nSuppose there exists an infinite set \\( S \\) of points in the plane where every pair of points has an integer distance, and not all points are colinear. Then, there exist at least three non-colinear points \\( A \\), \\( B \\), and \\( C \\).\n\n**Step 2: Fix a coordinate system.**  \nPlace \\( A \\) at \\((0, 0)\\) and \\( B \\) at \\((d, 0)\\), where \\( d \\) is an integer (the distance \\( AB \\)). Let \\( C \\) be at \\((x, y)\\) with \\( y \\neq 0 \\). The distances \\( AC \\) and \\( BC \\) are integers, so:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\in \\mathbb{Z}, \\quad \\sqrt{(x - d)^2 + y^2} \\in \\mathbb{Z}.\n\\]\nThese equations constrain \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) to satisfy Diophantine conditions.\n\n**Step 3: Analyze distances to a non-colinear point.**  \nSuppose there is a point \\( C \\not\\in AB \\). For any other point \\( D \\in S \\), the distances \\( CD \\), \\( AD \\), and \\( BD \\) must all be integers. If infinitely many points lie on \\( AB \\), consider a point \\( D \\) on \\( AB \\) at \\((a, 0)\\). The distance \\( CD \\) is:\n\\[\n\\sqrt{(a - x)^2 + y^2} \\in \\mathbb{Z}.\n\\]\nThis implies that for infinitely many integers \\( a \\), \\((a - x)^2 + y^2\\) must be a perfect square.\n\n**Step 4: Contradiction via number theory.**  \nFor fixed \\( x \\) and \\( y \\), the equation \\((a - x)^2 + y^2 = k^2\\) (where \\( k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\)) has only finitely many integer solutions \\( a \\). This is because the difference \\( k^2 - (a - x)^2 = y^2 \\) factors as \\((k - (a - x))(k + (a - x)) = y^2\\), and the number of factorizations of \\( y^2 \\) into two integers of the same parity is finite. Hence, there cannot be infinitely many \\( a \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\) satisfying this equation.\n\n**Step 5: Conclusion.**  \nIf \\( S \\) contains infinitely many points on \\( AB \\), the existence of \\( C \\not\\in AB \\) leads to a contradiction, as \\( C \\) can only be at an integer distance from finitely many points on \\( AB \\). Therefore, no such \\( C \\) can exist, and all points must lie on \\( AB \\).\n\n**Final Answer:**  \nAll points must lie on a single straight line.  \n\\boxed{\\text{All points lie on a single line.}}", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Assume Points Are Not Collinear**:\n   Suppose that points \\( A, B, C \\) are not collinear (i.e., they do not lie on the same line). We need to prove that there can only be a finite number of points \\( P \\) such that the distances from \\( P \\) to \\( A, B, \\) and \\( C \\) are all integers.\n\n2. **Define Maximum Distance \\( k \\)**:\n   Let \\( k \\) be the largest integer between the distances \\( AB, BC, \\) and \\( CA \\). Therefore, \\( k = \\max(AB, BC, CA) \\).\n\n3. **Constraint on Distance Differences**:\n   For any point \\( P \\), the distance differences between \\( P \\) and \\( A, B \\), denoted as \\( |PA - PB| \\), must satisfy:\n   \\[\n   |PA - PB| \\leq AB \\leq k\n   \\]\n\n4. **Geometric Place of Points \\( P \\)**:\n   The set of all points \\( P \\) such that \\( |PA - PB| = d \\) (where \\( d \\) is an integer between 0 and \\( k \\)) forms a hyperbola with foci at points \\( A \\) and \\( B \\). Since \\( d \\) can take any integer value from 0 to \\( k \\), point \\( P \\) must lie on one of the \\( k+1 \\) hyperbolas (one of which may degenerate into a line).\n\n5. **Intersecting Hyperbolas**:\n   Similarly, for the distances between \\( P \\) and points \\( B, C \\), the point \\( P \\) must lie on one of the \\( k+1 \\) hyperbolas with foci at points \\( B \\) and \\( C \\).\n\n6. **Intersection Properties of Hyperbolas**:\n   Now, consider the intersections of these hyperbolas:\n   - According to the properties of conic sections, any two hyperbolas can intersect at no more than four points.\n   - Hyperbolas with a common focus do not intersect at any points.\n\n7. **Counting Intersection Points**:\n   Since we have two sets of hyperbolas formed by points \\( A, B \\) and \\( B, C \\), and given the above constraints, the total number of intersection points of these hyperbolas is:\n   \\[\n   4(k+1)^2\n   \\]\n\n   This is the upper limit for the number of points \\( P \\) where all distances are integers (referring to \"Problem 31.074\").\n\n8. **Conclusion**:\n   Since the number of such points \\( P \\) is finite when \\( A, B, C \\) are not collinear, an infinite set of points where all distances are integers can only exist if all points lie on the same line.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{An infinite set of points with integer distances must lie on a straight line.}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Each resident of city \\( N \\) has acquaintances that make up at least 30% of the city's population. A resident will vote if at least one of their acquaintances is running for mayor. Prove that it is possible to conduct mayoral elections in city \\( N \\) with two candidates such that at least half of the residents will participate in the voting.", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I have this problem about a city where each resident has acquaintances making up at least 30% of the population. The task is to prove that we can have mayoral elections with two candidates such that at least half of the residents will vote. A resident votes if at least one of their acquaintances is running. Hmm, int