ast 51%, which is more than half. Therefore, that would solve the problem. Wait, is that right?\n\nWait, let's check that logic. If for each resident, the probability that they are not acquainted with a randomly chosen candidate is at most 0.7. Then for two candidates, the probability that they are not acquainted with either is at most 0.7 * 0.7 = 0.49. Therefore, the probability that they are acquainted with at least one is at least 0.51. Therefore, the expected number of residents covered is 0.51N. Since this is an expectation, there must exist some pair of candidates where the actual number is at least 0.51N, which is more than half. Hence, such a pair exists.\n\nTherefore, by the probabilistic method, we can argue that such two candidates exist. Therefore, the conclusion follows. But wait, is this a valid application of the probabilistic method?\n\nYes, because the expectation over all possible pairs is 0.51N, so there must be at least one pair that achieves at least 0.51N, which is more than half. Therefore, that pair suffices. So, that seems like a straightforward solution. But let me make sure I didn't skip any steps.\n\nWait, but the problem states that each resident has acquaintances making up at least 30% of the population. So, each resident's acquaintance set is at least 0.3N. Therefore, if we pick a candidate uniformly at random, the probability that a resident knows that candidate is at least 0.3. Since acquaintances are fixed, the only randomness is in the selection of the candidate. However, when selecting two candidates, if we pick them independently and uniformly at random, then the probability that a resident is not acquainted with either is at most (1 - 0.3)^2 = 0.49. So the expectation is indeed 0.51N.\n\nBut wait, are the two candidates selected independently? Or are they selected without replacement? Since we can't have the same candidate twice, right? So maybe if the two candidates are distinct, then the calculation is slightly different. Let'