), their combined acquaintances cover at least \\( 51\\% \\) of the residents. Thus, it is possible to ensure that at least half of the population participates in the election.  \n\\(\\boxed{\\text{Such two candidates exist, ensuring at least half of the residents vote.}}\\)", "ground_truth_solution": "\nLet's start with one of the solutions provided to prove the statement that at least half of the city's population will participate in the election.\n\n# I\n\nThis solution is based on the following lemma:\n\n**Lemma:** Let $S$ be an arbitrary nonempty set of residents. Then there's a resident in city $N$ who knows at least 30% of the residents from $S$.\n\nLet's denote the number of residents in a set $X$ by $|X|$. We will estimate the total number of ordered pairs of acquaintances $(t, s)$, where $t$ is an arbitrary person and $s$ is a person from $S$. For each $s_0 \\in S$, the number of pairs of the type $(t, s_0)$ is not less than $0.3 |N|$. Therefore, the total number of pairs is at least $0.3|S||N|$. \n\nThus, for some person $t_0$, the number of pairs of the type $(t_0, s)$ is not less than $0.3|S|$, which proves the lemma.\n\n**Election Process:**\n\n1. Assume we nominate an arbitrary resident $A$ as the first candidate.\n2. Consider the set $S$ of residents who are not acquainted with $A$.\n3. If the set $S$ is empty, then any other resident of the city $N$, distinct from $A$, can be nominated as the second candidate.\n4. If the set $S$ is non empty, using the lemma above, we can find a resident $B$ who is acquainted with at least 30% of the residents from $S$.\n\n**Participation Calculation:**\n\n- Let resident $A$ have $k$ acquaintances.\n- The total number of residents in $N$ is $n$.\n\nFor the solution provided:\n$$\\text{Number of residents participating} = k + 0.3 \\cdot (n - k) = 0.3n + 0.7k \\,.$$\n\nSince $k \\geq 0.3 n$, we get:\n$$0.3 n + 0.7 k \\geq 0.3 n + 0.7 \\cdot 0.3 n = 0.51 n\\,.$$\n\nHence, more than half the residents of city $N$ will participate in the elections. \n\nThus, the solution is complete.\n$$\\boxed{0.5n}$$\n\n\n# II\n\nLet's symbolically introduce parameters for the solution:\n\n1. Let $n$ be the number of residents in city $N$.\n2. We will calculate the number of residents acquainted with either of two residents, $A$ and $B$.\n\nThe main idea is to estimate the number of residents who are acquainted with either $A$ or $B$.\n\n**Calculation:**\n\n1. Calculate the number of pairs of residents, which is \n   $$ \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\,.$$\n\n2. For each resident $M$, estimate the number of pairs of residents where at least one is acquainted with $M$. \n\nKnowing each resident is acquainted with at least 30% of the others:\n- Remaining pairs in which both are not acquainted with $M$:\n  $$ \\frac{(n-0.3n)(n-0.3n-1)}{2} \\leq \\frac{0.49n^2 - 0.3n}{2}\\,.$$\n\n- The total number of pairs of acquaintances must satisfy the condition:\n  $$ \\frac{n(n-1)}{2}\\,.$$\n\nSince we need the significant overlap for the total number of pairs:\n$$0.5 n \\cdot \\frac{n(n-1)}{2} = \\frac{(0.5m^2 - 0.5n)}{2}\\,.$$\n\nCombining the results:\n$$\\text{Number pairs with known acquaintance} > n \\cdot \\frac{0.5n^2 - 0.5n}{2}\\,.$$\n\nTherefore:\n$$\\frac{0.5n(n-1)}{2} > \\frac{0.5 n^2 - 0.5 n}{2}$$\n\nIt concludes that there are sufficient pairs indicating friends and hence sufficient residents who would be participating in the election:\n$$0.51n \\ge 0.5n$$\n\nThus proving our desired statement.\n\n$$\\boxed{0.5n}$$", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "The tangents at \\( A \\) and \\( B \\) to the circumcircle of an acute triangle \\( ABC \\) intersect at \\( T \\). Point \\( D \\) lies on line \\( BC \\) such that \\( DA = DC \\). Prove that \\( TD \\parallel AC \\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that TD is parallel to AC in this geometry problem. Let me start by understanding the given information and visualizing the problem