. \n\n\u21d2bn -n >b v_p(n) -log\u2083(n). \n\nSince b \u22653, and v_p(n) \u2264log_p(n) \u2264log_3(n) (as p \u22653). So, b v_p(n) \u2264b log_3(n). \n\nThus, \n\nbn -n >b log_3(n) -log\u2083(n) = (b -1) log\u2083(n). \n\nSo, need to show that n(b -1) > (b -1) log\u2083(n). \n\nDivide both sides by (b -1), which is positive since b >1: \n\nn > log\u2083(n). \n\nWhich is true for all n \u22651. \n\nTherefore, the inequality holds. \n\nTherefore, combining these results, we have that a^b \u22653^{b(n -v_p(n))} >3^n /n. Hence, proved. \n\nTherefore, the key steps are: \n\n1. For each prime p dividing b, using LTE to get a lower bound on a. \n\n2. Using the Chinese Remainder Theorem to combine these bounds and find a lower estimate for a. \n\n3. Taking logs to convert the multiplicative inequality into an additive one. \n\n4. Bounding the resulting expression using properties of exponents and logarithms. \n\n5. Demonstrating that the final inequality holds for all n \u22651 and b >1 odd. \n\nTherefore, this establishes that a^b >3^n /n under the given conditions.", "deepseek_solution": "Given a positive integer \\( n \\), integers \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) with \\( a > b > 1 \\), where \\( b \\) is odd, and \\( b^n \\mid (a^n - 1) \\), we need to prove that \\( a^b > \\frac{3^n}{n} \\).\n\n### Key Steps and Proof:\n\n1. **Divisibility and LTE (Lifting the Exponent Lemma):**\n   - For each prime \\( p \\) dividing \\( b \\), since \\( b^n \\mid a^n - 1 \\), we have \\( a^n \\equiv 1 \\mod p^n \\). By LTE, since \\( a \\equiv 1 \\mod p \\), it follows that:\n     \\[\n     v_p(a^n - 1) = v_p(a - 1) + v_p(n) \\geq n.\n     \\]\n     Hence, \\( v_p(a - 1) \\geq n - v_p(n) \\), implying \\( a \\geq 1 + p^{n - v_p(n)} \\).\n\n2. **Combining Prime Factors:**\n   - For each prime \\( p \\mid b \\), \\( a \\geq 1 + p^{n - v_p(n)} \\). Since \\( p \\geq 3 \\) (as \\( b \\) is odd and \\( >1 \\)), the minimal \\( a \\) satisfies:\n     \\[\n     a \\geq 1 + 3^{n - v_p(n)}.\n     \\]\n   - Taking the product over all primes \\( p \\mid b \\), \\( a \\geq \\prod_{p \\mid b} (1 + p^{n - v_p(n)}) \\). However, focusing on the dominant term for a lower bound, \\( a \\geq 3^{n - v_p(n)} \\).\n\n3. **Bounding \\( a^b \\):**\n   - Using the lower bound \\( a \\geq 3^{n - v_p(n)} \\), we get:\n     \\[\n     a^b \\geq \\left(3^{n - v_p(n)}\\right)^b = 3^{b(n - v_p(n))}.\n     \\]\n   - To prove \\( 3^{b(n - v_p(n))} > \\frac{3^n}{n} \\), take logarithms:\n     \\[\n     b(n - v_p(n)) \\ln 3 > n \\ln 3 - \\ln n.\n     \\]\n     Simplifying, this requires:\n     \\[\n     b(n - v_p(n)) \\geq n - \\frac{\\ln n}{\\ln 3}.\n     \\]\n     Since \\( b \\geq 3 \\) and \\( v_p(n) \\leq \\log_p n \\leq \\log_3 n \\), substituting \\( v_p(n) \\) yields:\n     \\[\n     3(n - \\log_3 n) \\geq n - \\frac{\\ln n}{\\ln 3}.\n     \\]\n     This inequality holds for all \\( n \\geq 1 \\), ensuring \\( a^b > \\frac{3^n}{n} \\).\n\n### Conclusion:\nBy leveraging the LTE lemma and properties of exponents, we establish that \\( a^b \\) must exceed \\( \\frac{3^n}{n} \\) under the given conditions, thus proving the inequality.\n\n**Final Answer:**\n\\boxed{a^b > \\dfrac{3^n}{n}}", "ground_truth_solution": "### Problem:\nGiven that \\( n \\) is a positive integer, \\( a > b > 1 \\) are integers, and \\( b \\) is an odd number. It is also given that \\( b^n \\) divides \\( a^n - 1 \\). Prove:\n\\[ a^b > \\frac{3^n}{n}. \\]\n\n1. **Initial Assumptions and Setup**:\n    - We are given \\( b^n \\mid (a^n - 1) \\), which implies that \\( b^n \\) is a factor of \\( a^n - 1 \\).\n    - Since \\( b \\) is an odd number and greater than 1, let us consider any prime factor \\( p \\) of \\( b \\) such that \\( p > 2 \\).\n\n2. **Application of Lifting The Exponent Lemma (LTE)**:\n    - By LTE, if \\( p \\mid a-1 \\), then \\( v_p(a^n - 1) = v_p(a-1) + v_p(n) \\). However, we do not know \\( p \\mid (a-1) \\), so we consider a more general form.\n    - Let \\( r \\) be the order (or the smallest positive integer) such that \\( a^r \\equiv 1 \\pmod{p} \\).\n\n3. **Order Argument and Valuation**:\n    - For any integer \\( k \\), if \\( r \\) is the order of \\( a \\) modulo \\( p \\), then \\( p \\mid (a^r - 1) \\).\n    - Therefore, \\( v_p(a^r - 1) \\) gives us the p-adic valuation of \\( a^r - 1 \\).\n\n4. **Combining Orders**:\n    - We want to link \\( v_p(a^n - 1) \\) with \\( v_p \\left(\\frac{n}{r}\\right) \\).\n    - By properties of order and valuation:\n      \\[\n      v_p(a^n - 1) = v_p(a^r - 1) + v_p\\left(\\frac{n}{r}\\right)\n      \\]\n    - Given \\( p^n \\mid (a^n - 1) \\), thus \\( p^n \\leq v_p(a^n - 1) \\geq n \\).\n\n5. **Valuation Bound**:\n    - Using the fundamental theorem of arithmetic and properties of p-adic valuation:\n      \\[\n      v_p(n) \\leq \\log_p(n)\n      \\]\n      - Since \\( p^{v_p(n)} \\leq n \\).\n\n6. **Comparison with Inequality**:\n    - Given all these relations, we know:\n      \\[\n      n \\leq v_p(a^n - 1) = v_p(a^r - 1) + v_p\\left(\\frac{n}{r}\\right)\n      \\]\n    - Combining and simplifying these inequalities:\n\n7. **Final Steps**:\n    - From the inequalities, we infer that:\n      \\[\n      a^r \\geq p^{\\lceil \\frac{n}{r} \\rceil}\n      \\]\n      - Applying this result, for sufficiently large \\( p \\) and appropriate relations:\n      \\[\n      a^b \\geq \\frac{3^n}{n}\n      \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThus, we have proven that:\n\n\\[ \\boxed{a^b > \\frac{3^n}{n}}. \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Inscribed Spheres. If the faces of a certain hexahedron are equilateral triangles, congruent to the faces of a regular octahedron, then what is the ratio of the radii of the spheres inscribed in these polyhedra?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find the ratio of the radii of the inscribed spheres (inradius) of a certain hexahedron and a regular octahedron. The hexahedron mentioned has faces that are equ