s see.\n\nSuppose the pentagon is ABCDE. Each edge of the pentagon is part of a triangle. For edge AB, the triangle is ABF; for edge BC, triangle BCG; edge CD, triangle CDH; edge DE, triangle DEI; and edge EA, triangle EAJ. Now we have five new vertices F, G, H, I, J. But we only have three additional vertices. So this is too many. Therefore, we need to reuse some of these vertices.\n\nMaybe connect multiple triangles to the same vertex. For example, let F be the vertex for triangles ABF, BCG, and CDH. Wait, but that would require F to be connected to A, B, C, D, which might not form triangles properly.\n\nAlternatively, let F, G, H be arranged such that each is shared by multiple triangles. For example:\n\n- Vertex F is connected to A, B, C.\n- Vertex G is connected to C, D, E.\n- Vertex H is connected to E, A, B.\n\nBut let's check the degrees:\n\n- F is connected to A, B, C: degree 3 (odd)\n- G is connected to C, D, E: degree 3 (odd)\n- H is connected to E, A, B: degree 3 (odd)\n\nBut all additional vertices need to have even degrees (at least 4). So this doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, have each additional vertex connected to four pentagon vertices. For example:\n\n- F connected to A, B, C, D.\n- G connected to B, C, D, E.\n- H connected to A, D, E, B.\n\nBut this might create multiple edges between vertices. Let's check:\n\nEdges from F: A-F, B-F, C-F, D-F\nEdges from G: B-G, C-G, D-G, E-G\nEdges from H: A-H, D-H, E-H, B-H\n\nPentagon edges: A-B, B-C, C-D, D-E, E-A\n\nNow, check degrees:\n\n- A: connected to B, E (pentagon), F, H. Degree 4.\n- B: connected to A, C (pentagon), F, G, H. Degree 5. (Problem)\n- C: connected to B, D (pentagon), F, G. Degree 4.\n- D: connected to C, E (pentagon), F, G, H. Degree 5. (Problem)\n- E: connected to D, A (pentagon), G, H. Degree 4.\n- F: connected to A, B, C, D. Degree 4.\n- G: connected to B, C, D, E. Degree 4.\n- H: connected to A, B, D, E. Degree 4.\n\nSo vertices B and D have degree 5, which is odd. Not acceptable.\n\n