nd equation:\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - \\cos^2 \\alpha = \\frac{\\lambda + 2}{2} + 2 \\sin \\alpha \\]\n\nRewriting \\(\\cos^2 \\alpha\\) as \\(1 - \\sin^2 \\alpha\\):\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - (1 - \\sin^2 \\alpha) = \\frac{\\lambda + 2}{2} + 2 \\sin \\alpha \\]\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - 1 + \\sin^2 \\alpha = \\frac{\\lambda + 2}{2} + 2 \\sin \\alpha \\]\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - \\frac{\\lambda}{2} - 2 + \\sin^2 \\alpha - 2 \\sin \\alpha = 0 \\]\n\nCompleting the square for the \\(\\sin \\alpha\\) terms:\n\\[ \\sin^2 \\alpha - 2 \\sin \\alpha = (\\sin \\alpha - 1)^2 - 1 \\]\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - \\frac{\\lambda}{2} - 2 + (\\sin \\alpha - 1)^2 - 1 = 0 \\]\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - \\frac{\\lambda}{2} + (\\sin \\alpha - 1)^2 - 3 = 0 \\]\n\nSince \\((\\sin \\alpha - 1)^2 \\geq 0\\), we have:\n\\[ \\lambda^2 - \\frac{\\lambda}{2} \\leq 3 \\]\n\nSolving the quadratic inequality:\n\\[ 2\\lambda^2 - \\lambda - 6 \\leq 0 \\]\n\\[ \\lambda \\in \\left[-\\frac{3}{2}, 2\\right] \\]\n\nNext, we express \\(\\frac{\\lambda}{m}\\) in terms of \\(\\lambda\\):\n\\[ \\frac{\\lambda}{m} = \\frac{2\\lambda}{\\lambda + 2} \\]\n\nAnalyzing the function \\(f(\\lambda) = \\frac{2\\lambda}{\\lambda + 2}\\) over \\(\\lambda \\in \\left[-\\frac{3}{2}, 2\\right]\\):\n\n- When \\(\\lambda = -\\frac{3}{2}\\), \\(f\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right) = -6\\)\n- When \\(\\lambda = 2\\), \\(f(2) = 1\\)\n\nThe function \\(f(\\lambda)\\) is continuous and increasing on the interval \\(\\left[-\\frac{3}{2}, 2\\right]\\), thus the range of \\(\\frac{\\lambda}{m}\\) is \\([-6, 1]\\).\n\nThe range of \\(\\frac{\\lambda}{m}\\) is \\(\\boxed{[-6, 1]}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. We are given:\n   $$\\boldsymbol{a} = \\left(\\lambda + 2, \\lambda^2 - \\cos^2 \\alpha\\right)$$\n   $$\\boldsymbol{b} = \\left(m, \\frac{m}{2} + \\sin \\alpha\\right),$$\n   where \\(\\lambda\\), \\(m\\), and \\(\\alpha\\) are real numbers. We know that \\(\\boldsymbol{a} = 2 \\boldsymbol{b}\\).\n\n2. To find the relationship between \\(\\lambda\\) and \\(m\\), we need t