ssume the statement holds for all indices up to \\( m \\). Consider \\( m > 1 \\) and we analyze different scenarios based on the presence of \\(a_m\\):\n\n     1. **Case 1: \\(a_m\\) in the pair only:**\n        Suppose \\( a_m = a_i \\). Consider the other element in the pair, say \\( a_j \\).\n        \\[ 0 < |a_p + a_q \\pm a_r| \\leq 3a_{i-1} \\]\n        Since \\( a_m - 1 = (3a_{m-1})! \\), which divides the expression involving sums of smaller sequence elements, it holds by induction hypothesis that:\n        \\[ \\gcd(a_i + a_m, a_p + a_q \\pm a_r) = \\gcd( (a_i + 1) + (a_m - 1), a_p + a_q \\pm a_r) = \\gcd(a_i + 1, a_p + a_q \\pm a_r) = 1 \\]\n\n     2. **Case 2: \\( a_m \\) in the triple only:**\n        Suppose \\( a_m = a_q \\). Similar to case 1, the inductive hypothesis ensures that any divisor properties hold, meaning:\n        \\[ \\gcd(a_i + a_j, a_p + a_m \\pm a_r) = \\gcd(a_i + a_j, a_p + 1 \\pm a_r) = 1 \\]\n\n     3. **Case 3: \\( a_m \\) in both a pair and a triple:**\n        Suppose \\( a_m = a_j = a_q \\). Then similarly, as before:\n        \\[ \\gcd(a_i + a_m, a_p + a_m \\pm a_r) = \\gcd( (a_i + a_m), (a_p + a_m \\pm a_r)) - (a_i + a_m)) = \\gcd(a_i + 1, a_p - a_i \\pm a_r) = 1 \\]\n\nThus, the lemma holds by the principle of mathematical induction. Hence, we have shown that the defined sequence satisfies the required condition.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nWe have constructed an infinite increasing sequence of natural numbers \\(a_1, a_2, a_3, \\ldots\\) such that the sum of any two distinct members is coprime with the sum of any three distinct members. Thus, the answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{Yes}}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}]